      "math_academy_id": "e-46",
      "problem": "Compute $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} -5 \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^{1+x}.$",
      "worked_solution": "We can use the laws of exponents and the algebra of limits to rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }-5{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{1+x} & =-5{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{1+x} \\\\  & =-5{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x} \\\\  & =-5{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y = \\left(\\dfrac 1 3\\right)^x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14258364/assets/81be7f2983aeb647a8fa88e2caad8cea3c2052190222670838077e760a2e7fda.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function decreases to zero as $x$ increases. So\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty}   \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^{x} = 0,\n$$\n\nand finally, we have\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{5}{3}\\cdot \\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty}   \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^{x} = -\\dfrac 5 3\\cdot 0 = 0.\n$$",
